content:
Problem summary:
Observed intermittent "early EOF"/connection closed events from clients consuming SSE/streaming responses when payloads cross ~64KB boundary. Client sees truncated JSON chunk or connection resets mid-frame. Repro intermittent but correlated with high concurrency and nginx reverse-proxy in front of runtime.

Why this doc: scratchpad to capture commands I ran, minimal reproducer, logs, and short hypotheses so we don't re-run the same experiments.

Environment notes:
- Runtime: serving-runtime v0.31.2 (local dev image)
- Frontend proxy: nginx 1.22.1 (default proxy_pass config)
- Client tests run from macOS + Linux CI runner
- Observed with Node fetch clients and curl -N; Python requests (non-streaming) masks the issue.

Quick repro (minimal):
1) Start minimal SSE server (node) that emits a large JSON line > 70KB:

# server.js
const http = require('http');
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream', 'Cache-Control': 'no-cache', 'Connection': 'keep-alive' });
  const payload = { msg: 'x'.repeat(72000), t: Date.now() };
  res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(payload)}\n\n`);
  // keep connection open
}).listen(8083);

2) Start nginx proxy (simple config):
proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8083;
proxy_buffering on; # default

3) Client: curl -N http://localhost:8080/stream
Expected: a single event with full payload.
Observed (intermittent): curl exits early or prints truncated JSON (cut in middle), or shows "curl: (18) transfer closed with outstanding read data remaining".

Commands I used while diagnosing:
- curl -v -N --http1.1 localhost:8080/stream  # force http1.1, get verbose socket close
- socat - TCP4:localhost:8080,raw,echo=0  # watch raw bytes flow and closure
- tcpdump -i lo0 -s 0 -w sse-earlyeof.pcap port 8080  # capture and inspect in Wireshark
- ab -n 100 -c 20 -k http://localhost:8080/stream  # concurrency stress (keeps connections)

What I saw in nginx logs:
- upstream prematurely closed connection while reading response header from upstream
- "client prematurely closed connection" entries match times of curl complaints

Hypotheses (ordered):
1) nginx proxy_buffering default causes it to read chunks into buffer and on certain sizes flush/close upstream prematurely if upstream doesn't signal content-length (chunked transfer) and connection reuse triggers early close.
2) TLS/ALPN or HTTP/2 framing mismatch between runtime and proxy when payload crosses internal buffer thresholds.
3) Runtime's streaming implementation uses single write for large payload; node kernel TCP stack segments, and our keepalive/linger/socket timeout interactions at proxy cause RST when upstream socket returns EPIPE after proxy's assert.
4) KV cache/continuous batching step introduces small delay causing proxy timeout threshold to trigger.

Experiments and results (notes):
- Turning off nginx proxy_buffering (proxy_buffering off) reduced frequency dramatically (from ~30% to <5% in quick stress runs). Suggests buffering behaviour related.
- Forcing smaller writes from server (split payload into 4x ~18KB writes with 5ms setTimeout between writes) also reduced failures.
- Enabling chunked transfer explicitly (res.setHeader('Transfer-Encoding','chunked')) didn't change much; nginx still logs upstream prematurely closed at times unless buffering is off.
- Switching client to HTTP/2 (curl --http2) removed early EOF in my local tests (but prod uses http1.1 between nginx and upstream).

Minimal Node client repro (to reproduce truncated parsing):
// client.js
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
(async () => {
  const r = await fetch('http://localhost:8080/stream');
  const reader = r.body.getReader();
  let buf = '';
  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    buf += Buffer.from(value).toString('utf8');
    // try parse when we see full event separator
    if (buf.includes('\n\n')) {
      const event = buf.split('\n\n')[0];
      console.log('event chunk len', event.length);
      // attempt JSON parse of data: line
      const m = event.match(/^data: (.*)$/m);
      if (m) {
        try { JSON.parse(m[1]); console.log('parsed ok'); }
        catch (e) { console.error('parse failed', e.message); }
      }
      buf = ''; // drop
    }
  }
})();

Notes from parse failures: when truncated, JSON.parse throws 'Unexpected end of JSON input' (consistent).

Immediate mitigations suggested to prod: 
- Set proxy_buffering off for streaming endpoints in nginx config (quick and effective).
- Add explicit small-chunk writes in runtime for large-first-token responses (eg split message into 16KB writes).
- Add a runtime metric/export that counts "single-write > 50KB" responses to correlate to errors.

Next experiments to run:
- Reproduce with TLS between nginx->upstream to rule out ALPN/http1.1 interactions.
- Try increasing proxy_read_timeout and proxy_send_timeout to see if timeouts are culprit.
- Reproduce on dedicated environment with same nginx config as production (we use haproxy in some regions).

Notes for follow-up ticket (JIRA-2026-1429):
- Attach pcap if we can capture the RST/FIN pattern.
- Attach nginx error.log snippets and upstream logs with timestamps.
- Patch suggestion: in runtime, if response length > X, write as multi-chunk with small delays / or set TCP_CORK/CORK semantics where available.

Open questions:
- Why does HTTP/2 client not hit the issue locally? Probably different framing/flow-control semantics. Needs more testing.
- Does cloud LB (GCLB/ALB) vs nginx behave differently? We should test both.

Personal TODOs (quick):
- [ ] Add a small regression test in e2e harness that runs curl -N via nginx and ensures response contains valid JSON for 100 concurrent clients.
- [ ] Add instrumentation on runtime for write sizes and write counts per request.
- [ ] Run the early-eof reproducer against staging with haproxy in front.

If anyone picks this up: start with proxy_buffering off + split writes in runtime and rerun ab -k. Results I saw were convincing enough to push a short-term nginx config change for streaming endpoints.

End of notes.
